window.FE_MEDIA={"aldrin": {"id": "as11-40-5903", "title": "Astronaut Edwin Aldrin walks on lunar surface near leg of Lunar Module", "date": "1969-07-20", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS11-40-5903 (20 July 1969) --- Astronaut Edwin E. Aldrin Jr., lunar module pilot, walks on the surface of the moon near the leg of the Lunar Module (LM) \"Eagle\" during the Apollo 11 extravehicular activity (EVA). Astronaut Neil A. Armstrong, commander, took this photograph with a 70mm lunar surface camera. While astronauts Armstrong and Aldrin descended in the Lunar Module (LM) \"Eagle\" to explore the Sea of Tranquility region of the moon, astronaut Michael Collins, command module pilot, remained with the Command and Service Modules (CSM) \"Columbia\" in lunar orbit.", "keywords": ["ACTIVITY", "APOLLO 11", "APOLLO PROJECT", "ASTRONAUTS", "EXTRAVEHICULAR ACTIVITY", "EXTRAVEHICULAR MOBILITY UNITS", "LUNAR MODULE", "LUNAR PHOTOGRAPHY"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as11-40-5903/as11-40-5903~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as11-40-5903", "bytes": 496127, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1874, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1874}, "a11launch": {"id": "S69-39961", "title": "Liftoff of the Apollo 11 lunar landing mission", "date": "1969-07-16", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "S69-39961 (16 July 1969) --- The huge, 363-feet tall Apollo 11 (Spacecraft 107/Lunar Module S/Saturn 506) space vehicle is launched from Pad A, Launch Complex 39, Kennedy Space Center (KSC), at 9:32 a.m. (EDT), July 16, 1969. Onboard the Apollo 11 spacecraft are astronauts Neil A. Armstrong, commander; Michael Collins, command module pilot; and Edwin E. Aldrin Jr., lunar module pilot.  Apollo 11 is the United States' first lunar landing mission. While astronauts Armstrong and Aldrin descend in the Lunar Module (LM) \"Eagle\" to explore the Sea of Tranquility region of the moon, astronaut Collins will remain with the Command and Service Modules (CSM) \"Columbia\" in lunar orbit. Photo credit: NAS", "keywords": [], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/S69-39961/S69-39961~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/S69-39961", "bytes": 331127, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1329, "h": 1920, "origW": 1329, "origH": 1920}, "surveyor3": {"id": "as12-48-7121", "title": "View of Surveyor III in its crater", "date": "1969-11-20", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS12-48-7121 (20 Nov. 1969) --- An excellent view of the unmanned Surveyor 3 spacecraft which was photographed during the Apollo 12 second extravehicular activity (EVA) on the surface of the moon. The Apollo 12 Lunar Module (LM), with astronauts Charles Conrad Jr., commander, and Alan L. Bean, lunar module pilot, aboard landed within 600 feet of Surveyor 3 in the Ocean of Storms. The television camera and several other pieces were taken from Surveyor 3 and brought back to Earth for scientific examination. Surveyor 3 landed on the side of this small crater in the Ocean of Storms on April 19, 1967. Astronaut Richard F. Gordon Jr., command module pilot, remained with the Apollo 12 Command and S", "keywords": ["APOLLO 12 FLIGHT", "APOLLO PROJECT", "CRATERS", "LUNAR EXPLORATION", "LUNAR MODULE", "LUNAR PROBES", "LUNAR SURFACE", "SURVEYOR 3 LUNAR PROBE"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as12-48-7121/as12-48-7121~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as12-48-7121", "bytes": 611878, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}, "ranger7": {"id": "PIA02975", "title": "First Image of the Moon taken by a U.S. Spacecraft", "date": "2000-11-04", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "Ranger 7 took this image, the first picture of the Moon by aU.S. spacecraft, on 31 July 1964 at 13:09 UT 9:09 AM EDT about 17 minutes before impacting the lunar surface.", "keywords": ["Moon", "Ranger 7"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA02975/PIA02975~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA02975", "bytes": 94244, "ext": "jpg", "w": 720, "h": 707, "origW": 720, "origH": 707}, "lunokhod": {"id": "PIA12967", "title": "Soviet Union Lunar Rovers", "date": "2010-03-17", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University", "desc": "This image taken by NASA Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter shows Soviet robotic lander Luna 17 still sitting on Mare Imbrium where it delivered the Lunokhod 1 Rover in November 1970.", "keywords": ["Moon", "Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter LRO"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA12967/PIA12967~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA12967", "bytes": 144473, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1000, "h": 1000, "origW": 1000, "origH": 1000}, "luna21": {"id": "PIA12976", "title": "Luna 21 Lander", "date": "2010-03-19", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University", "desc": "Luna 21 lander delivered the Lunokhod 2 rover to the floor of Le Monnier crater in January 1973. This image taken by NASA Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter also shows the rover tracks.", "keywords": ["Moon", "Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter LRO"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA12976/PIA12976~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA12976", "bytes": 25468, "ext": "jpg", "w": 450, "h": 450, "origW": 450, "origH": 450}, "m3": {"id": "PIA12158", "title": "Moon Mapper Looks Homeward", "date": "2009-08-03", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL/Brown", "desc": "NASA Moon Minerology Mapper, a guest instrument onboard the Indian Space Research Organization Chandrayaan-1 mission to the moon, looks homeward. Australia is visible in the lower center of the image.", "keywords": ["Moon", "Chandrayaan-1"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA12158/PIA12158~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA12158", "bytes": 48937, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1709, "h": 669, "origW": 1709, "origH": 669}, "moonnear": {"id": "PIA12235", "title": "Nearside of the Moon", "date": "2009-09-24", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "ISRO/NASA/JPL-Caltech/Brown Univ.", "desc": "Nearside of the Moon", "keywords": ["Moon", "Chandrayaan-1"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA12235/PIA12235~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA12235", "bytes": 254636, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 1400, "origW": 2000, "origH": 1400}, "lro": {"id": "PIA12086", "title": "Loading Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter LRO in the Thermal Vacuum Chamber", "date": "2009-07-07", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "Loading the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter into the thermal vacuum chamber at Goddard Space Flight Center. Diviner is one of seven instruments aboard NASA LRO Mission.", "keywords": ["Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter LRO"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA12086/PIA12086~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA12086", "bytes": 43621, "ext": "jpg", "w": 456, "h": 342, "origW": 456, "origH": 342}, "artemis1launch": {"id": "NHQ202211160030", "title": "Artemis I Launch", "date": "2022-11-16", "center": "HQ", "photographer": "NASA/Joel Kowsky", "desc": "NASA’s Space Launch System rocket carrying the Orion spacecraft launches on the Artemis I flight test, Wednesday, Nov. 16, 2022, from Launch Complex 39B at NASA’s Kennedy Space Center in Florida. NASA’s Artemis I mission is the first integrated flight test of the agency’s deep space exploration systems: the Orion spacecraft, Space Launch System (SLS) rocket, and ground systems. SLS and Orion launched at 1:47 a.m. EST, from Launch Pad 39B at the Kennedy Space Center. Photo Credit: (NASA/Joel Kowsky)", "keywords": ["Artemis", "Artemis I", "Artemis I Launch", "Cape Canaveral", "Florida", "Kennedy Space Center", "Launch Complex 39B", "Orion"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/NHQ202211160030/NHQ202211160030~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ202211160030", "bytes": 415886, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}, "orion_earth_moon": {"id": "art001e001822", "title": "Flight Day 13: Earth, Array, and Moon", "date": "2022-11-28", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "art001e001822 (Nov. 28, 2022) On flight day 13 of the Artemis I mission, Orion captured this view of Earth and the Moon on either sides of one of the spacecraft’s four solar arrays.", "keywords": ["Artemis I Resource Reel", "Artemis I", "Artemis", "Orion", "Moon", "Earth"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/art001e001822/art001e001822~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/art001e001822", "bytes": 200621, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1440, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1440}, "earthset2026": {"id": "art002e009284", "title": "Earthset Over the Lunar Limb", "date": "2026-04-06", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "NASA", "desc": "art002e009284 (April 6, 2026) – Earth appears tiny as the Moon looms large in this photo taken by the Artemis II crew during their lunar flyby on April 6, 2026.   Taken 36 minutes before Earthset, our home planet is visible in the blackness of space off the limb of the illuminated Moon. Earth is in a crescent phase, with sunlight coming from the right. Orientale mare basin, with its dark floor of cooled lava and outer rings of mountains, covers nearly the lower third of the imaged lunar surface. Different colors in the mare hint at its mineral composition. The lines of small indentations above Orientale are secondary crater chains, formed by material ejected during a violent primary impact. ", "keywords": ["Artemis", "Artemis II", "Moon", "Earth", "Lunar Flyby", "Orion", "Earthset"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/art002e009284/art002e009284~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/art002e009284", "bytes": 67676, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1280, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1280}, "artemis2pad": {"id": "Artemis II at the pad Moon 01292026_4", "title": "Moon Seen behind Full Artemis II Stack at Launch Pad 39B", "date": "2026-01-29", "center": "MSFC", "photographer": "NASA/Sam Lott", "desc": "The Moon is seen behind the SLS (Space Launch System) and Orion spacecraft, atop the mobile launcher on January 28, 2026. The rocket is currently at Launch Pad 39B at NASA’s Kennedy Space Center in Florida, as teams are preparing for a wet dress rehearsal to practice timelines and procedures for the launch of Artemis II. 508 Description:The Moon is seen shining over the SLS (Space Launch System) and Orion spacecraft, atop the mobile launcher on January 29, 2026. The rocket is currently at Launch Pad 39B at NASA’s Kennedy Space Center in Florida, as teams are preparing for a wet dress rehearsal to practice timelines and procedures for the launch of Artemis II.", "keywords": ["Artemis II", "Moon", "Kennedy Space Center", "Space Launch System", "SLS"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/Artemis II at the pad Moon 01292026_4/Artemis II at the pad Moon 01292026_4~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/Artemis%20II%20at%20the%20pad%20Moon%2001292026_4", "bytes": 333787, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1280, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1280}, "voyager_test": {"id": "PIA21735", "title": "Voyager Test Model Configuration", "date": "2017-07-05", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "This archival photo shows the Voyager proof test model, which did not fly in space, in the 25-foot space simulator chamber at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory on December 3, 1976.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21735", "keywords": ["Voyager", "test model"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21735/PIA21735~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21735", "bytes": 475307, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1875, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1875}, "voyager_launch": {"id": "PIA21747", "title": "Voyager 1 Launch", "date": "1977-09-05", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/KSC", "desc": "NASA's Voyager 1 spacecraft launched atop its Titan/Centaur-6 launch vehicle from the Kennedy Space Center Launch Complex in Florida on September 5, 1977, at 8:56 a.m. local time.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21747", "keywords": ["Voyager", "Titan/Centaur-6"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21747/PIA21747~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21747", "bytes": 249026, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1591, "h": 2000, "origW": 1833, "origH": 2304}, "golden_record": {"id": "PIA21741", "title": "Voyager: Preparing the Golden Record", "date": "2017-07-05", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "The Voyager 1 Golden Record is prepared for installation on the spacecraft in this archival image from 1977.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21741", "keywords": ["Voyager", "golden record"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21741/PIA21741~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21741", "bytes": 321958, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1581, "h": 2000, "origW": 1760, "origH": 2227}, "paleblue": {"id": "PIA00452", "title": "Solar System Portrait - Earth as Pale Blue Dot", "date": "1996-09-12", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "This narrow-angle color image of the Earth, dubbed Pale Blue Dot, is a part of the first ever 'portrait' of the solar system taken by NASA’s Voyager 1. The spacecraft acquired a total of 60 frames for a mosaic of the solar system from a distance of more than 4 billion miles from Earth and about 32 degrees above the ecliptic. From Voyager's great distance Earth is a mere point of light, less than the size of a picture element even in the narrow-angle camera. Earth was a crescent only 0.12 pixel in size. Coincidentally, Earth lies right in the center of one of the scattered light rays resulting from taking the image so close to the sun. This blown-up image of the Earth was taken through three ", "keywords": ["Earth", "Voyager", "pale blue dot"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA00452/PIA00452~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA00452", "bytes": 35200, "ext": "jpg", "w": 453, "h": 614, "origW": 453, "origH": 614}, "jupiter": {"id": "PIA22946", "title": "Jupiter Marble", "date": "2019-03-21", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "", "desc": "This striking view of Jupiter's Great Red Spot and turbulent southern hemisphere was captured by NASA's Juno spacecraft as it performed a close pass of the gas giant planet.  Juno took the three images used to produce this color-enhanced view on Feb. 12, 2019, between 9:59 a.m. PST (12:59 p.m. EST) and 10:39 p.m. PST (1:39 p.m. EST), as the spacecraft performed its 17th science pass of Jupiter. At the time the images were taken, the spacecraft was between 16,700 miles (26,900 kilometers) and 59,300 miles (95,400 kilometers) above Jupiter's cloud tops, above a southern latitude spanning from about 40 to 74 degrees. https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA22946  Enhanced image by Kevin M.", "keywords": ["Juno", "Jupiter"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA22946/PIA22946~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22946", "bytes": 196969, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}, "juno_crescent": {"id": "PIA25013", "title": "A View of Crescent Jupiter from Juno", "date": "2022-02-22", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "mage data: NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS     Image processing by Kevin M. Gill © CC BY", "desc": "If you could ride along with NASA's Juno spacecraft as it approaches Jupiter during one of its regular close passes by the giant planet, you would be treated to a striking vista similar to this one.  Unlike the Moon or Venus, this view of Jupiter in a crescent phase is impossible to see from Earth, even using a telescope. Since Jupiter's orbit is outside Earth's, an observer on Earth can only see the side of Jupiter that is illuminated by the Sun, so the planet always appears full.  Citizen scientist Kevin M. Gill created this mosaic using raw data from the JunoCam instrument. It comprises seven images taken during Juno's 39th close pass by Jupiter on Jan. 12, 2022.  https://photojournal.jpl", "keywords": ["Juno", "Jupiter"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA25013/PIA25013~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA25013", "bytes": 51188, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1080, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1080}, "neptune": {"id": "PIA02210", "title": "Neptune", "date": "1999-08-19", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "This contrast enhanced color picture of Neptune was acquired by NASA Voyager 2 on Aug. 14, 1989. As Voyager 2 approached Neptune, rapidly increasing image resolution is revealed striking new details. Bright, wispy clouds are seen overlying the Great Dar", "keywords": ["Neptune", "Voyager"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA02210/PIA02210~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA02210", "bytes": 37743, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1000, "h": 1000, "origW": 1000, "origH": 1000}, "uranus": {"id": "PIA18182", "title": "Uranus as seen by NASA Voyager 2", "date": "1986-12-18", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "This is an image of the planet Uranus taken by the spacecraft Voyager 2 in 1986.", "keywords": ["Uranus", "Voyager"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA18182/PIA18182~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA18182", "bytes": 83489, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1720, "h": 1720, "origW": 1720, "origH": 1720}, "cassini_earth": {"id": "PIA21345", "title": "So Far from Home", "date": "2017-09-11", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute", "desc": "With this view, Cassini captured one of its last looks at Saturn and its main rings from a distance. The Saturn system has been Cassini's home for 13 years, but that journey is nearing its end.  Cassini has been orbiting Saturn for nearly a half of a Saturnian year but that journey is nearing its end. This extended stay has permitted observations of the long-term variability of the planet, moons, rings, and magnetosphere, observations not possible from short, fly-by style missions.  When the spacecraft arrived at Saturn in 2004, the planet's northern hemisphere, seen here at top, was in darkness, just beginning to emerge from winter. Now at journey's end, the entire north pole is bathed in t", "keywords": ["Cassini-Huygens", "Saturn", "S ring"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21345/PIA21345~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21345", "bytes": 1548258, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 1035, "origW": 3545, "origH": 1834}, "saturn_v": {"id": "PIA00400", "title": "Saturn and 4 Icy Moons in Natural Color", "date": "1998-06-08", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL/USGS", "desc": "This approximate natural-color image shows Saturn, its rings, and four of its icy satellites. Three satellites (Tethys, Dione, and Rhea) are visible against the darkness of space, and another smaller satellite (Mimas) is visible against Saturn's cloud tops very near the left horizon and just below the rings. The dark shadows of Mimas and Tethys are also visible on Saturn's cloud tops, and the shadow of Saturn is seen across part of the rings. Saturn, second in size only to Jupiter in our Solar System, is 120,660 km (75,000 mi) in diameter at its equator (the ring plane) but, because of its rapid spin, Saturn is 10% smaller measured through its poles. Saturn's rings are composed mostly of ice", "keywords": ["Voyager 2", "Tethys", "Dione", "Rhea"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA00400/PIA00400~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA00400", "bytes": 47248, "ext": "jpg", "w": 860, "h": 1230, "origW": 860, "origH": 1230}, "cassini_assembly": {"id": "PIA04603", "title": "Cassini Spacecraft in a JPL Assembly Room", "date": "2003-07-02", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "On October of 1997, a two-story-tall robotic spacecraft will begin a journey of many years to reach and explore the exciting realm of Saturn, the most distant planet that can easily be seen by the unaided human eye. In addition to Saturn's interesting atmosphere and interior, its vast system contains the most spectacular of the four planetary ring systems, numerous icy satellites with a variety of unique surface features. A huge magnetosphere teeming with particles that interact with the rings and moons, and the intriguing moon Titan, which is slightly larger than the planet Mercury, and whose hazy atmosphere is denser than that of Earth, make Saturn a fascinating planet to study.  The Cassi", "keywords": ["Saturn", "Cassini-Huygens"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA04603/PIA04603~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA04603", "bytes": 267661, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1485, "h": 1457, "origW": 1485, "origH": 1457}, "cassini_noodle": {"id": "PIA21622", "title": "Cassini Near-Infrared \"Noodle\" Mosaic of Saturn", "date": "2017-07-24", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute", "desc": "This mosaic of 30 images was captured by NASA's Cassini spacecraft on June 29, 2017, as it raced toward the gap between Saturn and its rings during one of 22 close passes over Saturn that comprise the mission's final phase, called the Grand Finale.  For the first frame of the mosaic, Cassini's camera was pointed toward a location at approximately 80 degrees north latitude, as the spacecraft was flying 16,000 miles (26,000 kilometers) above the top of the clouds at 45 degrees north latitude. When the last frame was captured, the orbiter was 8,000 miles (13,000 kilometers) above 30 degrees north latitude and looking straight down at the planet.  The images in this sequence were captured in nea", "keywords": ["Cassini-Huygens", "Saturn"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21622/PIA21622~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21622", "bytes": 43281, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1025, "h": 1920, "origW": 1025, "origH": 1920}, "hubble_sts125": {"id": "s125e006948", "title": "Hubble Space Telescope (HST) during rendezvous with STS-125 Shuttle Atlantis", "date": "2009-05-13", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "S125e006948 (13 May 2009) ---  An STS-125 crewmember onboard the Space Shuttle Atlantis snapped a still photo of the Hubble Space Telescope following grapple of the giant observatory by the shuttle?s Canadian-built remote manipulator system.", "keywords": [], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/s125e006948/s125e006948~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/s125e006948", "bytes": 302829, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1311, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1311}, "hubble_sts31": {"id": "s31-04-027", "title": "Hubble Space Telescope (HST) above OV-103's PLB during STS-31 deployment", "date": "1990-04-25", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "The Hubble Space Telescope (HST) is raised above the payload bay (PLB) in low hover position during STS-31 checkout and pre-deployment procedures aboard Discovery, Orbiter Vehicle (OV) 103. Stowed along the HST Support System Module (SSM) are the high gain antenna (HGA) (center) and the two solar arrays (one either side). In the background are the orbital maneuvering system (OMS) pods and the Earth's surface.", "keywords": ["ANTENNAS", "DISCOVERY (ORBITER)", "EARTH LIMB", "HUBBLE SPACE TELESCOPE", "STS-31", "PAYLOAD BAY", "PAYLOAD DEPLOYMENT & RETRIEVAL SYSTEM", "SOLAR ARRAYS"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/s31-04-027/s31-04-027~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/s31-04-027", "bytes": 357815, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1258, "h": 1920, "origW": 1258, "origH": 1920}, "webb_mirror": {"id": "GSFC_20161102_2016-21507_012", "title": "James Webb Space Telescope Mirror Reveal", "date": "2016-11-02", "center": "GSFC", "photographer": "NASA/GSFC/Bill Hrybyk", "desc": "James Webb Space Telescope Mirror Reveal", "keywords": ["James Webb Space Telescope Mirror Reveal"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GSFC_20161102_2016-21507_012/GSFC_20161102_2016-21507_012~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20161102_2016-21507_012", "bytes": 366702, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1483, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1483}, "webb_carina": {"id": "carina_nebula", "title": "James Webb Space Telescope NIRCam Image of the “Cosmic Cliffs” in Carina Nebula", "date": "2022-07-12", "center": "STScI (Webb)", "photographer": "NASA ESA CSA STScI", "desc": "What looks much like craggy mountains on a moonlit evening is actually the edge of a nearby, young, star-forming region NGC 3324 in the Carina Nebula. Captured in infrared light by the Near-Infrared Camera (NIRCam) on NASA’s James Webb Space Telescope, this image reveals previously obscured areas of star birth.  Called the Cosmic Cliffs, the region is actually the edge of a gigantic, gaseous cavity within NGC 3324, roughly 7,600 light-years away. The cavernous area has been carved from the nebula by the intense ultraviolet radiation and stellar winds from extremely massive, hot, young stars located in the center of the bubble, above the area shown in this image. The high-energy radiation fro", "keywords": ["NGC 3324", "Carina Nebula"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/carina_nebula/carina_nebula~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/carina_nebula", "bytes": 389076, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1111, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1111}, "sojourner": {"id": "PIA04318", "title": "Sojourner Sits Near \"Rock Garden\"", "date": "2003-02-01", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "The Mars Pathfinder Rover Sojourner images by the Imager for Mars Pathfinder as it nears the rock \"Wedge.\" Part of the Rock Garden is visible in the upper right of the image.  http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA04318", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mars Pathfinder (MPF)"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA04318/PIA04318~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA04318", "bytes": 84678, "ext": "jpg", "w": 819, "h": 415, "origW": 819, "origH": 415}, "pathfinder": {"id": "PIA01121", "title": "Sojourner Rover View of Pathfinder Lander", "date": "1997-12-12", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "Sojourner Rover View of Pathfinder Lander", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mars Pathfinder MPF"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA01121/PIA01121~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA01121", "bytes": 18772, "ext": "jpg", "w": 411, "h": 190, "origW": 411, "origH": 190}, "curiosity": {"id": "PIA19808", "title": "Looking Up at Mars Rover Curiosity in Buckskin Selfie", "date": "2015-08-19", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS", "desc": "This low-angle self-portrait of NASA's Curiosity Mars rover shows the vehicle at the site from which it reached down to drill into a rock target called \"Buckskin\" on lower Mount Sharp.  The selfie combines several component images taken by Curiosity's Mars Hand Lens Imager (MAHLI) on Aug. 5, 2015, during the 1,065th Martian day, or sol, of the rover's work on Mars. For scale, the rover's wheels are 20 inches (50 centimeters) in diameter and about 16 inches (40 centimeters) wide. This view is a portion of a larger panorama available at PIA19807.  A close look reveals a small rock stuck onto Curiosity's left middle wheel (on the right in this head-on view). The rock had been seen previously du", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mars Science Laboratory MSL", "MAHLI"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA19808/PIA19808~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA19808", "bytes": 285001, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1169, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1169}, "gale": {"id": "PIA16029", "title": "Gale Crater Vista, in Glorious Color", "date": "2012-08-09", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS", "desc": "This is the first 360-degree panorama in color of the Gale Crater landing site taken by NASA Curiosity rover. The panorama was made from thumbnail versions of images taken by the Mast Camera.", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mars Science Laboratory MSL"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA16029/PIA16029~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA16029", "bytes": 276917, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 413, "origW": 3653, "origH": 755}, "perseverance": {"id": "PIA24542", "title": "Perseverance's Selfie with Ingenuity", "date": "2021-04-07", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS", "desc": "NASA's Perseverance Mars rover took a selfie with the Ingenuity helicopter, seen here about 13 feet (3.9 meters) from the rover in this image taken April 6, 2021, the 46th Martian day, or sol, of the mission by the WATSON (Wide Angle Topographic Sensor for Operations and eNgineering) camera on the SHERLOC (Scanning Habitable Environments with Raman and Luminescence for Organics and Chemicals) instrument, located at the end of the rover's long robotic arm.  Perseverance's selfie with Ingenuity is made up of 62 individual images stitched together once they are sent back to Earth; they were taken in sequence while the rover was looking at the helicopter, then again while it was looking at the W", "keywords": ["Mars 2020", "Mars"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA24542/PIA24542~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24542", "bytes": 370840, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1412, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1412}, "ingenuity": {"id": "PIA23882", "title": "NASA's Ingenuity Mars Helicopter", "date": "2020-04-29", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "The flight model of NASA's Ingenuity Mars Helicopter.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA23882", "keywords": ["Mars 2020"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA23882/PIA23882~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA23882", "bytes": 108828, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1280, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1280}, "spirit": {"id": "PIA05040", "title": "Spirit Stretches Out", "date": "2004-01-10", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "This frame from an animation flips back and forth between images taken before and after deployment of the Mars Exploration Rover Spirit's bogie, a part of the rover's suspension system that extends the wheel base. These images were taken by Spirit's hazard avoidance camera.  An animation is available at http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA05040", "keywords": ["Mars Exploration Rover (MER)", "Spirit"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA05040/PIA05040~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA05040", "bytes": 290611, "ext": "jpg", "w": 900, "h": 900, "origW": 900, "origH": 900}, "opportunity": {"id": "PIA08812", "title": "The Opportunity Rover at Victoria Crater", "date": "2006-10-06", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL/University of Arizona", "desc": "The Opportunity Rover at Victoria Crater", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mars Exploration Rover MER,Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter MRO"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA08812/PIA08812~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA08812", "bytes": 163786, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1176, "h": 942, "origW": 1176, "origH": 942}, "viking": {"id": "PIA00563", "title": "First Color Image From Viking Lander 1", "date": "1997-02-28", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "First Color Image From Viking Lander 1", "keywords": ["Mars", "Viking"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA00563/PIA00563~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA00563", "bytes": 57141, "ext": "jpg", "w": 563, "h": 512, "origW": 563, "origH": 512}, "viking2": {"id": "PIA00396", "title": "Viking Lander 2 First Picture On The Surface Of Mars", "date": "1996-12-12", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL", "desc": "Viking Lander 2 First Picture On The Surface Of Mars  http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA00396", "keywords": ["Mars", "Viking"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA00396/PIA00396~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA00396", "bytes": 205684, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1614, "h": 512, "origW": 1614, "origH": 512}, "mariner4": {"id": "PIA14032", "title": "First TV Image of Mars", "date": "2011-04-12", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/Dan Goods", "desc": "This is an enhanced contrast version of the first Mars photograph released on July 15, 1965. This is man first close-up photograph of another planet, a photographic representation of digital data radioed from Mars by the Mariner 4 spacecraft.", "keywords": ["Mars", "Mariner Mars 1964 Mariner 4"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA14032/PIA14032~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14032", "bytes": 266248, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1209, "h": 1280, "origW": 1209, "origH": 1280}, "insight": {"id": "PIA23203", "title": "InSight's Dusty Selfie", "date": "2019-05-06", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "This is NASA InSight's second full selfie on Mars. Since taking its first selfie, the lander has removed its heat probe and seismometer from its deck, placing them on the Martian surface; a thin coating of dust now covers the spacecraft as well.  This selfie is a mosaic made up of 14 images taken on March 15 and April 11 - the 106th and 133rd Martian days, or sols, of the mission - by InSight's Instrument Deployment Camera, located on its robotic arm.  InSight's first selfie showed its instruments still on the deck. Now that they're removed, the viewer can see the spacecraft's air pressure sensor (white object in center), the tether box for its seismometer and the tether for its heat probe r", "keywords": ["InSight", "Mars"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA23203/PIA23203~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA23203", "bytes": 193327, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1096, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1096}, "maven": {"id": "PIA14754", "title": "Primary Structure for MAVEN Spacecraft", "date": "2011-09-26", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/Lockheed Martin", "desc": "NASA Mars Atmosphere and Volatile EvolutioN MAVEN spacecraft is scheduled to launch in November 2013 and will be the first mission devoted to understanding the Martian upper atmosphere.", "keywords": ["Mars Atmosphere and Volatile Evolution MissioN MAVEN"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA14754/PIA14754~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14754", "bytes": 317483, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1536, "h": 1920, "origW": 1536, "origH": 1920}, "phoenix": {"id": "PIA11379", "title": "Underneath the Phoenix Lander", "date": "2008-10-29", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Max Planck Institute", "desc": "Underneath the Phoenix Lander", "keywords": ["Mars", "Phoenix"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA11379/PIA11379~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA11379", "bytes": 28623, "ext": "jpg", "w": 512, "h": 256, "origW": 512, "origH": 256}, "mro": {"id": "PIA07212", "title": "Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter in High Bay", "date": "2005-01-07", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL/Lockheed Martin", "desc": "Engineers and technicians at Lockheed Martin Space Systems, Denver, building the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter for NASA.", "keywords": ["Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter MRO"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA07212/PIA07212~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA07212", "bytes": 79259, "ext": "jpg", "w": 500, "h": 767, "origW": 500, "origH": 767}, "mercury": {"id": "PIA13823", "title": "MESSENGER Explores Mercury - In Color", "date": "2011-01-25", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Carnegie Institution of Washington", "desc": "MESSENGER Explores Mercury - In Color", "keywords": ["Mercury", "MESSENGER"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA13823/PIA13823~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA13823", "bytes": 981635, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 723, "origW": 4425, "origH": 1600}, "venus": {"id": "PIA23791", "title": "Venus from Mariner 10", "date": "2020-06-08", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/JPL-Caltech", "desc": "As it sped away from Venus, NASA's Mariner 10 spacecraft captured this seemingly peaceful view of a planet the size of Earth, wrapped in a dense, global cloud layer. But, contrary to its serene appearance, the clouded globe of Venus is a world of intense heat, crushing atmospheric pressure and clouds of corrosive acid.  This newly processed image revisits the original data with modern image processing software. A contrast-enhanced version of this view, also provided here, makes features in the planet's thick cloud cover visible in greater detail.  The clouds seen here are located about 40 miles (60 kilometers) above the planet's surface, at altitudes where Earth-like atmospheric pressures an", "keywords": ["Mariner 10", "Venus"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA23791/PIA23791~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA23791", "bytes": 94315, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 976, "origW": 2245, "origH": 1096}, "pluto": {"id": "PIA20202", "title": "New Horizons Very Best View of Pluto", "date": "2015-12-05", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Southwest Research Institute", "desc": "This frame from a movie is composed of the sharpest views of Pluto that NASA's New Horizons spacecraft obtained during its flyby of the distant planet on July 14, 2015. The pictures are part of a sequence taken near New Horizons' closest approach to Pluto, with resolutions of about 250-280 feet (77-85 meters) per pixel -- revealing features smaller than half a city block on Pluto's diverse surface. The images include a wide variety of spectacular, cratered, mountainous and glacial terrains -- giving scientists and the public alike a breathtaking, super-high resolution window on Pluto's geology.  The images form a strip 50 miles (80 kilometers) wide trending from Pluto's jagged horizon about ", "keywords": ["Pluto", "New Horizons"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA20202/PIA20202~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA20202", "bytes": 97140, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1280, "h": 720, "origW": 1280, "origH": 720}, "bennu": {"id": "DSCN0120", "title": "OSIRIS-REx Bennu Sample Arrives at NASA Goddard", "date": "2023-11-23", "center": "GSFC", "photographer": "Molly Wasser", "desc": "Photographed inside a laboratory at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland, this cannister contains a portion of the asteroid Bennu sample delivered to Earth by the agency's OSIRIS-REx (Origins, Spectral Interpretation, Resource Identification, and Security – Regolith Explorer) mission.", "keywords": ["OSIRIS-REx", "Asteroid Bennu"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/DSCN0120/DSCN0120~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/DSCN0120", "bytes": 180976, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1440, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1440}, "iss": {"id": "s134e010137", "title": "Overall view of ISS taken afer STS-134 Undocking", "date": "2011-05-30", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "S134-E-010137 (29 May 2011) --- The International Space Station is featured in this image photographed by an STS-134 crew member on the space shuttle Endeavour after the station and shuttle began their post-undocking relative separation. Undocking of the two spacecraft occurred at 11:55 p.m. (EDT) on May 29, 2011. Endeavour spent 11 days, 17 hours and 41 minutes attached to the orbiting laboratory. Photo credit: NASA", "keywords": [], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/s134e010137/s134e010137~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/s134e010137", "bytes": 299021, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1275, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1275}, "sputnik": {"id": "9248168", "title": "Early Rockets", "date": "1957-10-04", "center": "MSFC", "photographer": "", "desc": "The Army Ballistic Missile Agency incorporated the von Braun team in key positions with Dr. von Braun as a head of the Development Operations Division. On October 4, 1957, the Nation was shocked when the Russians launched Sputnik, the world's first artificial satellite. Two months later, the United States suffered disappointment when a Navy Vanguard rocket, with its satellite payload, failed to develop sufficient thrust and toppled over on the launch pad.", "keywords": ["Sputnik", "Russians", "Artificial Satellite"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/9248168/9248168~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/9248168", "bytes": 4641944, "ext": "jpg", "w": 2000, "h": 1274, "origW": 3000, "origH": 1911}, "juno_grs": {"id": "PIA21376", "title": "Crescent Jupiter with the Great Red Spot", "date": "2017-01-13", "center": "JPL", "photographer": "", "desc": "This image of a crescent Jupiter and the iconic Great Red Spot was created by a citizen scientist (Roman Tkachenko) using data from Juno's JunoCam instrument. You can also see a series of storms shaped like white ovals, known informally as the \"string of pearls.\" Below the Great Red Spot a reddish long-lived storm known as Oval BA is visible.  The image was taken on Dec. 11, 2016 at 2:30 p.m. PST (5:30 p.m. EST), as the Juno spacecraft performed its third close flyby of Jupiter. At the time the image was taken, the spacecraft was about 285,100 miles (458,800 kilometers) from the planet.  http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21376  .  -    Enhanced image by Roman Tkachenko (CC-BY) base", "keywords": ["Jupiter", "Juno", "great red spot", "JunoCam"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21376/PIA21376~orig.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21376", "bytes": 35454, "ext": "jpg", "w": 825, "h": 1206, "origW": 825, "origH": 1206}, "orion_moon": {"id": "art001e000269", "title": "Orion Approaches Moon for Outbound Powered Flyby", "date": "2022-11-21", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "(Nov. 21, 2022) A portion of the Moon looms large just beyond the Orion spacecraft in this image taken on the sixth day of the Artemis I mission by a camera on the tip of one of Orion’s solar arrays. The spacecraft entered the lunar sphere of influence Sunday, Nov. 20, making the Moon, instead of Earth, the main gravitational force acting on the spacecraft. On Monday,  Nov. 21, it came within 80 miles of the lunar surface, the closest approach of the uncrewed Artemis I mission, before moving into a distant retrograde orbit around the Moon.", "keywords": ["Artemis", "Artemis I", "Orion", "Artemis I resource reel"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/art001e000269/art001e000269~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/art001e000269", "bytes": 249175, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1440, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1440}, "hero": {"id": "as17-148-22727", "title": "View of the Earth seen by the Apollo 17 crew traveling toward the moon", "date": "1972-12-07", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS17-148-22727 (7 Dec. 1972) --- This view of Earth was seen by the Apollo 17 crew as they traveled toward the moon on their NASA lunar landing mission. This outstanding trans-lunar coast photograph extends from the Mediterranean Sea area to the Antarctica south polar ice cap. This is the first time the Apollo trajectory made it possible to photograph the south polar ice cap. Note the heavy cloud cover in the Southern Hemisphere. Almost the entire coastline of Africa is clearly visible. The Arabian Peninsula can be seen at the northeastern edge of Africa. The large island off the coast of Africa is the Malagasy Republic. The Asian mainland is on the horizon toward the northeast. The Apollo 1", "keywords": ["APOLLO 17 FLIGHT", "APOLLO PROJECT", "EARTH (PLANET)", "ONBOARD ACTIVITIES", "EARTH OBSERVATIONS (FROM SPACE)", "PHOTOGRAPHY"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as17-148-22727/as17-148-22727~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as17-148-22727", "bytes": 350623, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}, "earthrise": {"id": "as08-14-2383", "title": "Apollo 8 Mission image, Earth over the horizon of the moon", "date": "1968-12-24", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS08-14-2383 (24 Dec. 1968) --- The rising Earth is about five degrees above the lunar horizon in this telephoto view taken from the Apollo 8 spacecraft near 110 degrees east longitude. The horizon, about 570 kilometers (350 statute miles) from the spacecraft, is near the eastern limb of the moon as viewed from Earth. Width of the view at the horizon is about 150 kilometers (95 statute miles). On Earth 240,000 statute miles away the sunset terminator crosses Africa. The crew took the photo around 10:40 a.m. Houston time on the morning of Dec. 24, and that would make it 15:40 GMT on the same day. The South Pole is in the white area near the left end of the terminator. North and South America ", "keywords": ["APOLLO 8", "APOLLO PROJECT", "EARTH LIMB", "LUNAR ORBITS", "LUNAR PHOTOGRAPHS", "MOON", "LUNAR SURFACE", "ONBOARD ACTIVITIES"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as08-14-2383/as08-14-2383~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as08-14-2383", "bytes": 139142, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}, "flag": {"id": "as11-40-5875", "title": "Astronaut Edwin Aldrin poses for photograph beside deployed U.S. flag", "date": "1969-07-20", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS11-40-5875 (20 July 1969) --- Astronaut Edwin E. Aldrin Jr., lunar module pilot of the first lunar landing mission, poses for a photograph beside the deployed United States flag during an Apollo 11 extravehicular activity (EVA) on the lunar surface. The Lunar Module (LM) is on the left, and the footprints of the astronauts are clearly visible in the soil of the moon. Astronaut Neil A. Armstrong, commander, took this picture with a 70mm Hasselblad lunar surface camera. While astronauts Armstrong and Aldrin descended in the LM, the \"Eagle\", to explore the Sea of Tranquility region of the moon, astronaut Michael Collins, command module pilot, remained with the Command and Service Modules (CSM", "keywords": ["APOLLO 11", "APOLLO PROJECT", "ASTRONAUTS", "EXTRAVEHICULAR ACTIVITY", "LUNAR MODULE EXTRAVEHICULAR MOBILITY UNITS", "LUNAR EXPLORATION", "LUNAR LANDING SITES", "MOON LUNAR PHOTOGRAPHY"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as11-40-5875/as11-40-5875~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as11-40-5875", "bytes": 496516, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1856, "h": 1920, "origW": 1856, "origH": 1920}, "easep": {"id": "as11-40-5948", "title": "Astronaut Edwin Aldrin after deployment of EASEP on surface of moon", "date": "1969-07-20", "center": "JSC", "photographer": "", "desc": "AS11-40-5948 (20 July 1969) --- Astronaut Edwin E. Aldrin Jr., lunar module pilot, is photographed during the Apollo 11 extravehicular activity (EVA) on the moon. He has just deployed the Early Apollo Scientific Experiments Package (EASEP). This is a good view of the deployed equipment. In the foreground is the Passive Seismic Experiment Package (PSEP); beyond it is the Laser Ranging Retro-Reflector (LR-3); in the center background is the United States flag; in the left background is the black and white lunar surface television camera; in the far right background is the Lunar Module (LM). Astronaut Neil A. Armstrong, commander, took this picture with a 70mm lunar surface camera. While astron", "keywords": ["APOLLO 11", "APOLLO PROJECT", "ASTRONAUTS", "EXTRAVEHICULAR ACTIVITY", "LUNAR MODULE LASER RANGE FINDERS", "LUNAR PHOTOGRAPHY", "LUNAR SURFACE", "PHOTOGRAPHY PHOTOGRAPHIC EQUIPMENT"], "src": "https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as11-40-5948/as11-40-5948~large.jpg", "page": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/as11-40-5948", "bytes": 493612, "ext": "jpg", "w": 1920, "h": 1920, "origW": 1920, "origH": 1920}};
